    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">First episode of psychosis with marked mood symptoms, provisional diagnosis (F29)</content>. No psychotic or manic symptoms on risperidone, with a depressive phase easing. Dr. Langford agrees that the history points most strongly to bipolar I disorder with psychotic features but that the order of the mood and psychotic symptoms is not yet clear, so the diagnosis stays provisional. To prevent another episode she advises lithium, the first choice for long-term prevention. Baseline creatinine 0.72 mg/dL, eGFR 116, TSH 2.1 mIU/L and calcium 9.4 mg/dL are normal, and a urine pregnancy test today is negative. Valproate is avoided because of its risks in pregnancy. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm; the safety plan and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline are in her phone; lithium will be dispensed in small amounts while her mood is low. [S]<list><item>Start lithium carbonate 300 mg twice daily.</item><item>Lithium level 5 to 7 days after starting, 12 hours after the evening dose.</item><item>Continue risperidone 2 mg for now and change one medicine at a time.</item><item>Enroll in the coordinated specialty care program.</item><item>Use condoms reliably; discuss a long-acting method at the next visits.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Secondary amenorrhea (N91.1)</content>. Periods stopped about four months before the admission, during very heavy training at a low weight, which points to low energy availability; prolactin 62 ng/mL on risperidone may now be adding to it. Pregnancy test negative. [S]<list><item>Repeat prolactin at the next check; if it stays high and her periods do not return, switch from risperidone to an antipsychotic that spares prolactin.</item><item>Sports dietitian appointment arranged; no dancing until she has seen the dietitian.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antipsychotic dose, adherence, and response</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Risperidone 2 mg; full adherence; no psychotic or manic symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Manic, depressive, and psychotic symptoms, and early warning signs</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No manic or psychotic symptoms; depressive phase easing</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Akathisia, parkinsonism, and sedation</td><td>Every visit in the first months and after any dose change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Morning sedation; no akathisia or parkinsonism</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale (AIMS)</td><td>Baseline, then every 6 months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Baseline AIMS score 0, done with the consultation</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and BMI</td><td>Every visit for 6 months after starting or changing an antipsychotic (CMID: baseline, 4, 8, and 12 weeks, then quarterly)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>51.0 kg (BMI 18.7); waist 66 cm</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure</td><td>Baseline, 12 weeks, then yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>106/66</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Fasting glucose and A1c</td><td>Baseline, 12 weeks to 4 months, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Glucose 84 mg/dL and A1c 5.0% at intake; next at 12 weeks to 4 months</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Fasting lipid panel</td><td>Baseline, 12 weeks to 4 months, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal at intake; next at 12 weeks to 4 months</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Prolactin</td><td>When there are symptoms of high prolactin, such as missed periods</td><td>Up to date</td><td>62 ng/mL at intake with absent periods; repeat at the next check</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Electrocardiogram</td><td>Before lithium if over 40 or with heart disease; before an antipsychotic with cardiac risk factors</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Age 29, no heart disease, fainting, or family history of sudden death</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Creatinine and eGFR on lithium</td><td>Baseline, every 2 to 3 months for 6 months, then every 6 to 12 months</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Baseline creatinine 0.72 mg/dL, eGFR 116, a week ago</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>TSH on lithium</td><td>Baseline, once or twice in the first 6 months, then every 6 to 12 months</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Baseline TSH 2.1 mIU/L a week ago</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Calcium on lithium</td><td>Baseline, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Baseline calcium 9.4 mg/dL a week ago</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lithium level (12-hour trough)</td><td>5 to 7 days after starting and after any change until stable, then every 3 to 6 months</td><td>Due</td><td>Lithium started today; level ordered for 5 to 7 days from now</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Lithium side effects and sick-day rules</td><td>At the start and every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Tremor, thirst, nausea and signs of toxicity explained; take with food; keep drinking normally; no anti-inflammatory painkillers</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Pregnancy prevention on lithium; valproate avoided</td><td>Before starting and at each visit until a reliable method is in place</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Urine pregnancy test negative today; valproate avoided; the small risk of heart defects with lithium early in pregnancy explained; condoms reliably for now</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression measure (PHQ-9)</td><td>Monthly during acute treatment, every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation, then periodically</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 9, from 11; item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review, safety plan, lethal means, and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm; safety plan reviewed; no firearm access; lithium dispensed two weeks at a time</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sleep</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Nine or ten hours on risperidone; regular times agreed</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Eating, exercise, and periods</td><td>Every visit while periods are absent or weight is low</td><td>Completed today</td><td>SCOFF 1 of 5 a week ago; three small meals; not dancing; periods absent; dietitian arranged</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Psychiatry consultation</td><td>At the start of treatment for a first episode</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Langford today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Coordinated specialty care engagement</td><td>Ongoing, for up to two to three years</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Enrolled today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>No manic, depressive or psychotic episode and no readmission through six months. In progress; target 1930-10-26 [S]</item><item>Lithium trough level 0.6 to 0.8 mmol/L with normal kidney and thyroid tests at six months. In progress; target 1930-10-26 [S]</item><item>Periods return and prolactin is normal by three months. In progress; target 1930-08-10 [S]</item><item>BMI at least 19, with food matched to her training, by six months. In progress; target 1930-10-26 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 score below 5 by six months. In progress; target 1930-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Canadian Network for Mood and Anxiety Treatments (CANMAT) and International Society for Bipolar Disorders (ISBD) 2018 guidelines for the management of patients with bipolar disorder (2018); The CANMAT and ISBD Guidelines for the Treatment of Bipolar Disorder: Summary and a 2023 Update of Evidence (2023); The American Psychiatric Association Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients With Schizophrenia, Third Edition (2020); Consensus development conference on antipsychotic drugs and obesity and diabetes (2004); Recovery After an Initial Schizophrenia Episode (RAISE): coordinated specialty care for first-episode psychosis (2026); U.S. Selected Practice Recommendations for Contraceptive Use, 2024 (2024); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Lithium level in 5 to 7 days at the laboratory. Visit in three weeks with lithium level, creatinine, electrolytes, prolactin, and the PHQ-9. Care coordinator weekly; dietitian next week. Next scheduled visit 1930-06-08: lithium level after the sick visit, prolactin, and the antipsychotic plan. [S]</paragraph></text>

